(defxdoc+ uniquify-evaluation
  :parents (unique-names-validation)
  :short "Evaluation is invariant under alpha-relatedness:
          the expression level."
  :long
  (xdoc::topstring
   (xdoc::p
    "This book carries out the main induction over the
     @(see eval-exprs/atoms/binds) clique for the validation of
     @(tsee expr-uniquify-names): if two ASTs are alpha-related under a
     renaming bundle (see @(tsee expr-alpha-related-p)) and satisfy the
     freshness side conditions (see @(tsee expr-alpha-fresh-p)), and the
     two dynamic environments are alpha-related via a witness (see
     @(tsee expr-denv-alpha-related-via-p)), have their keys supported by
     the freshness relation's support set and the renamings (see
     @(tsee expr-denv-keys-supported-p)), and satisfy the
     failure-direction relation at the free variables of the AST (see
     @(tsee expr-denv-alpha-avoided-p)), then the two evaluations err
     together and, on success, yield alpha-equivalent values (see
     @('expr-value-alpha-equiv-p')).")
   (xdoc::p
    "The statements are phrased as universally quantified predicates on
     the clique's own formals (one per function of the clique), so that
     the induction hypotheses can be instantiated at the renamed
     counterparts that each case constructs; their conclusions use the
     existential forms of the value relations, so that no witness
     needs to be computed alongside evaluation --- each case builds the
     witness of its result from the witnesses its hypotheses provide.")
   (xdoc::p
    "The book proceeds bottom-up: facts about related values (kinds,
     dimensions, the shape machinery of applications), the
     environment lookups and extensions under the relations, one lemma
     per case of the evaluator, and finally the induction.  Its top-level
     instance, and the main theorem that it yields, are in @(see
     unique-names-validation)."))
  :order-subtopics t
  :default-parent t)

